Implementation & Ethics Memo
How I Actually Used AI While Building
For the marketing website I used Loveable for the build out and Magic Patterns to create artifacts showing what the product actually looks like. Loveable had a built in AI feature using ChatGPT to power a basic chatbot. For the MVP automation I used N8N with an agent running ChatGPT 4.0.
The AI was mainly focused on code generation and design for the marketing website. The automation itself was built more manually. I wrote the system prompts myself. The agent does the task but in terms of how AI was used in building out the N8N process it was pretty light.
Human judgment was most important on the design side. Loveable and Magic Patterns did okay jobs with one shotting design and feature fragments but getting the website to where I was happy with it took quite a bit of fine tuning. The prompting to get it to look and feel right was iterative. AI got me 80% of the way there incredibly quickly but that final 20% still takes as long as it ever has. Making sure it is not buggy. Making sure it is designed right. Making sure it has the functionality you want. The prototyping process is incredibly quick with a real good feedback loop. But when you are actually trying to take a project to the final stages it is still not fully there.
Why the AI Feature in Our Product Looks the Way It Does
For the marketing website there were not many AI features I could add that would have been value add outside of a basic chatbot. I did not want the website to lose its actual functionality which is to communicate a message. Adding features that might slow load times or distract from the core messaging felt unnecessary. The chatbot itself I would probably replace with something like an Intercom Fin Agent once we actually had users on the product who needed customer support.
For the automation the agent is actually the core tool in our product. In a vacuum the agent is not that helpful. But the fact that I was able to link it up with other tools like Typeform, Google Sheets, and eventually Slack meant the agent was just one part in the process of being able to transform data into something useful.
We scoped down the automation for practicality. To get a working MVP that showed we could get customer information, transform it in some way, and provide something useful off it we needed actual customer data. We were hoping to use HubSpot, Intercom, and Stripe data to more fully flesh out the predictive churn analysis. So we scoped down to just the churned customer survey to demonstrate that we could take information and transform it in a particular way without actually having access to the customer data we need.
Our core value proposition is on the predictive churn analysis. In terms of showing that we can do this it is a technical feasibility question. Our next step is actually just getting access to real customer data.
Risks, Trade Offs, and Integrity
I chose to limit AI in the marketing website because I did not think it added a lot of value. I was more concerned with the actual messaging, copy, and design over having AI built in explicitly. The chatbot is there but it is not the point of the site.
In terms of privacy, bias, and over reliance on AI these concerns did not come into the forefront of our minds as we were building. We are trying to solve a very specific problem and the AI was just the best tool that allowed us to do that. The risks and trade offs feel minimal for a product like ours at this stage.
That said I recognize this will change as we scale. Once we are working with real customer data from HubSpot, Intercom, and Stripe we will need to think much more carefully about data privacy and security. Predictive churn analysis by definition requires access to sensitive customer behavior data. We will need to be clear with customers about what data we are ingesting, how we are using it, and what the model is actually predicting. The fact that the risks feel minimal now is a function of us not yet having access to the data that would make them real.
In terms of academic integrity and honest use of AI in my own work on this project I used AI extensively for code generation and design. I wrote the system prompts myself. The strategic decisions about what to build, why, and how were mine. AI was a tool not a substitute for thinking.
What I Learned About Building with GenAI
The biggest surprise was how much the best tool for the job changed throughout the semester. I went from using ChatGPT to Gemini to Claude as they released new models. What was best for code generation in September was not best by November. So I would tell another founder to keep trying the tools. Do not write anyone off permanently based on one model being better than another at any given time. The landscape shifts fast.
The other thing I would teach another founder is to expect the 80/20 split. AI gets you most of the way there incredibly quickly. But the last 20% of polish, debugging, and getting things right still takes real time and human judgment. Do not plan your timeline assuming AI will get you all the way to launch.
In terms of how I think about AI in my own future ventures it has to be a core part of the product. The amount of products now that have incredibly low technical moats just because of the speed you can get things working with AI means the bar for what problems are worth solving is higher. It is very difficult to think of products that will be the most helpful when the generally available models are already good at most given tasks. The competitive advantage has to come from somewhere other than the AI itself. Proprietary data. Unique workflows. Deep customer relationships. Something the model alone cannot replicate.

